{"timestamp":"2026-11-08T17:04:22.924Z","ordinal":44922,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1b054-fe0d-76c1-a45c-4726d155f0fa","turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-df03ccd2-f6b4-4779-b69b-2bd2495e9c1a","process_id":"90442","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","PATH=\"$PWD/tmp/ci-critical-path/tools/bin:/Users/kingsonwu/.rustup/toolchains/1.99.0-aarch64-apple-darwin/bin:$PATH\" RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.0 CARGO_TARGET_DIR=\"$PWD/target/ci-causal-canonical\" CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 REDACTED REDACTED caffeinate -i node scripts/validation/stage.mjs --help"],"cwd":"file:///Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk/.worktrees/ci-critical-path","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"PATH=\"$PWD/tmp/ci-critical-path/tools/bin:/Users/kingsonwu/.rustup/toolchains/1.99.0-aarch64-apple-darwin/bin:$PATH\" RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.0 CARGO_TARGET_DIR=\"$PWD/target/ci-causal-canonical\" CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 REDACTED REDACTED caffeinate -i node scripts/validation/stage.mjs --help"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"failed","aggregated_output":"Usage: stage.mjs <name> [--artifact path] [--resource name] [--context text] [--deadline epoch-ms] [--result path] -- command [args]\n","exit_code":1,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":6334}},"started_at_ms":1794157462924,"completed_at_ms":1794157462924}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-08T17:04:23.061Z","ordinal":44923,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1b054-fe0d-76c1-a45c-4726d155f0fa","turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-73fa8db3-592f-46c8-b8c1-daae52edfe3d","process_id":"68988","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat /Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat /Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"---\nname: labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite\ndescription: >-\n  Generate a clear conventional commit message from current git changes, stage\n  all files, commit locally, and run clean_commit.sh to normalize the result.\n  Use when users want to commit, stage and commit, auto-commit, or generate a\n  commit message for current changes. Trigger phrases: \"commit changes\", \"git\n  commit\", \"commit all\", \"commit message\", \"conventional commit\".\nlicense: MIT\nallowed-tools: \"Bash(git:*), Bash(npm:*), Bash(bash:*)\"\nmetadata:\n  pattern: pipeline\n  compatibility: \"macOS / Linux; requires git in PATH; runs in AI agent environment with shell access\"\n---\n\n# labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite\n\n## NEVER\n\n- **Never fabricate diff content** or invent changes not present in `git diff` output — a fabricated commit message is worse than no message; it destroys trust in git history.\n- **Never amend a previous commit** — always create a new commit. Amending rewrites history and can silently overwrite commits that others (or hooks) have already acted on.\n- **Never use `--no-verify`** to bypass pre-commit hooks — the hooks are the project's safety net; bypassing them defeats their purpose and masks real issues.\n- **Never write a body that only lists filenames** — `- update core.ts` tells nothing; name the intent behind the change, not the file that changed.\n\n## Commit Type Selection\n\n| Type | When to use |\n|------|-------------|\n| `feat` | New capability or behavior visible to users/agents |\n| `fix` | Corrects wrong behavior (not just wrong output) |\n| `docs` | Documentation, examples, references only — no behavior change |\n| `chore` | Housekeeping: structure, config, scripts, CI — no behavior change |\n| `refactor` | Code restructure with no behavior change |\n| `improve` | Meaningful enhancement to existing behavior (not a new feature, not a bug fix) |\n| `perf` | Performance improvement |\n| `test` | Test additions or changes only |\n\n**Scope selection:** use a scope when the change is confined to one clear subsystem — `fix(browser): ...`, `feat(skill): ...`. Omit scope for cross-cutting changes that span multiple subsystems.\n\n**When types conflict:** if a commit spans both a behavior change and a structural change, use the behavior-change type (`feat`/`fix`/`improve`) — structural changes are secondary to their purpose. If the two concerns are truly independent, split into two commits.\n\n## Execution Contract\n\n1. Inspect working tree changes (`git status --short`, `git diff --name-status`) before writing message.\n2. Before writing the subject, determine: is this a behavior change (`feat`/`fix`/`improve`) or a structural change (`docs`/`chore`/`refactor`)? This choice drives the type.\n   Generate a commit message that is both clear and elegant:\n   - Subject format: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>` or `<type>: <subject>`\n   - Subject is concise, action-oriented, and specific to the dominant change set.\n   - Body contains 2-6 bullet points, each tied to real grouped changes from diff.\n   - Avoid vague wording like only `update`/`align` when multiple meaningful changes exist.\n   - **MANDATORY — load `examples/good.md` before writing the message** to calibrate quality. Load `examples/anti-pattern.md` if the draft message feels vague or generic.\n3. Stage all changes with `git add -A`.\n4. Commit once with the generated subject and body. The commit command MUST be prefixed with `LABALI_SKILL_COMMIT=1` so the repository's pre-commit hook recognizes it as skill-authorized:\n   ```bash\n   LABALI_SKILL_COMMIT=1 git commit -m \"...\"\n   ```\n5. MUST run `<skill_base_dir>/scripts/clean_commit.sh` immediately after commit (non-optional).\n   The skill base directory is provided in the `Base directory for this skill:` line at the top of the invocation header.\n   `clean_commit.sh` may rewrite the subject — read the final hash and subject from its output, not from the original commit.\n\n## Message Quality Rules\n\n- Body bullets must reflect actual file-level intent, not generic placeholders.\n- Prefer aggregate summaries when many files changed, then include concrete sub-actions in body.\n\n## Failure Handling\n\n- If repository is not a Git repo, fail fast with explicit message.\n- If there are no changes to commit, return `No changes to commit.`.\n\n## Resources\n\n| When | Must load | Do NOT load |\n|------|-----------|-------------|\n| Always — before writing the message | `examples/good.md` | `examples/anti-pattern.md` |\n| Draft feels vague, generic, or uncertain | `examples/anti-pattern.md` | — |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":5667}},"started_at_ms":1794157463061,"completed_at_ms":1794157463061}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-08T17:04:23.111Z","ordinal":44924,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1bc1e-4647-7ad3-8c28-a5b670dfcd2b","call_id":"call_4e970301ee1d42e3abe5ab796a75f985","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"8dbb82\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000006334,\"exit_code\":1,\"original_token_count\":34,\"output\":\"Usage: stage.mjs <name> [--artifact path] [--resource name] [--context text] [--deadline epoch-ms] [--result path] -- command [args]\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"07d2c7\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005667,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1134,\"output\":\"---\\nname: labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite\\ndescription: >-\\n  Generate a clear conventional commit message from current git changes, stage\\n  all files, commit locally, and run clean_commit.sh to normalize the result.\\n  Use when users want to commit, stage and commit, auto-commit, or generate a\\n  commit message for current changes. Trigger phrases: \\\"commit changes\\\", \\\"git\\n  commit\\\", \\\"commit all\\\", \\\"commit message\\\", \\\"conventional commit\\\".\\nlicense: MIT\\nallowed-tools: \\\"Bash(git:*), Bash(npm:*), Bash(bash:*)\\\"\\nmetadata:\\n  pattern: pipeline\\n  compatibility: \\\"macOS / Linux; requires git in PATH; runs in AI agent environment with shell access\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite\\n\\n## NEVER\\n\\n- **Never fabricate diff content** or invent changes not present in `git diff` output — a fabricated commit message is worse than no message; it destroys trust in git history.\\n- **Never amend a previous commit** — always create a new commit. Amending rewrites history and can silently overwrite commits that others (or hooks) have already acted on.\\n- **Never use `--no-verify`** to bypass pre-commit hooks — the hooks are the project's safety net; bypassing them defeats their purpose and masks real issues.\\n- **Never write a body that only lists filenames** — `- update core.ts` tells nothing; name the intent behind the change, not the file that changed.\\n\\n## Commit Type Selection\\n\\n| Type | When to use |\\n|------|-------------|\\n| `feat` | New capability or behavior visible to users/agents |\\n| `fix` | Corrects wrong behavior (not just wrong output) |\\n| `docs` | Documentation, examples, references only — no behavior change |\\n| `chore` | Housekeeping: structure, config, scripts, CI — no behavior change |\\n| `refactor` | Code restructure with no behavior change |\\n| `improve` | Meaningful enhancement to existing behavior (not a new feature, not a bug fix) |\\n| `perf` | Performance improvement |\\n| `test` | Test additions or changes only |\\n\\n**Scope selection:** use a scope when the change is confined to one clear subsystem — `fix(browser): ...`, `feat(skill): ...`. Omit scope for cross-cutting changes that span multiple subsystems.\\n\\n**When types conflict:** if a commit spans both a behavior change and a structural change, use the behavior-change type (`feat`/`fix`/`improve`) — structural changes are secondary to their purpose. If the two concerns are truly independent, split into two commits.\\n\\n## Execution Contract\\n\\n1. Inspect working tree changes (`git status --short`, `git diff --name-status`) before writing message.\\n2. Before writing the subject, determine: is this a behavior change (`feat`/`fix`/`improve`) or a structural change (`docs`/`chore`/`refactor`)? This choice drives the type.\\n   Generate a commit message that is both clear and elegant:\\n   - Subject format: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>` or `<type>: <subject>`\\n   - Subject is concise, action-oriented, and specific to the dominant change set.\\n   - Body contains 2-6 bullet points, each tied to real grouped changes from diff.\\n   - Avoid vague wording like only `update`/`align` when multiple meaningful changes exist.\\n   - **MANDATORY — load `examples/good.md` before writing the message** to calibrate quality. Load `examples/anti-pattern.md` if the draft message feels vague or generic.\\n3. Stage all changes with `git add -A`.\\n4. Commit once with the generated subject and body. The commit command MUST be prefixed with `LABALI_SKILL_COMMIT=1` so the repository's pre-commit hook recognizes it as skill-authorized:\\n   ```bash\\n   LABALI_SKILL_COMMIT=1 git commit -m \\\"...\\\"\\n   ```\\n5. MUST run `<skill_base_dir>/scripts/clean_commit.sh` immediately after commit (non-optional).\\n   The skill base directory is provided in the `Base directory for this skill:` line at the top of the invocation header.\\n   `clean_commit.sh` may rewrite the subject — read the final hash and subject from its output, not from the original commit.\\n\\n## Message Quality Rules\\n\\n- Body bullets must reflect actual file-level intent, not generic placeholders.\\n- Prefer aggregate summaries when many files changed, then include concrete sub-actions in body.\\n\\n## Failure Handling\\n\\n- If repository is not a Git repo, fail fast with explicit message.\\n- If there are no changes to commit, return `No changes to commit.`.\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n| When | Must load | Do NOT load |\\n|------|-----------|-------------|\\n| Always — before writing the message | `examples/good.md` | `examples/anti-pattern.md` |\\n| Draft feels vague, generic, or uncertain | `examples/anti-pattern.md` | — |\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","create_time":1794157463.111326}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
