{"timestamp":"2026-11-08T17:04:37.313Z","ordinal":44933,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1b054-fe0d-76c1-a45c-4726d155f0fa","turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","session_id":"01a1b054-fe0d-76c1-a45c-4726d155f0fa","root_turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","response_id":"resp_0130d18d47a22683016af0aba2282087d0ba7de8d576ee575c","usage":{"input_tokens":44832,"cached_input_tokens":44544,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":124,"reasoning_output_tokens":9,"total_tokens":44956},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":474899539,"cached_input_tokens":471052928,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1265808,"reasoning_output_tokens":906464,"total_tokens":476165347},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1083136565,"cached_input_tokens":1074022400,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3100432,"reasoning_output_tokens":2178874,"total_tokens":1086236997}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-08T17:04:37.442Z","ordinal":44934,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1b054-fe0d-76c1-a45c-4726d155f0fa","turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-5c5d569b-73fd-4173-854b-3429124d554c","process_id":"91149","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","tail -n 12 tmp/ci-critical-path/replay-coordinator-local.log; sed -n '1,85p' docs/ai-coding-reliability.md; cat /Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/examples/good.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk/.worktrees/ci-critical-path","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"tail -n 12 tmp/ci-critical-path/replay-coordinator-local.log","name":"replay-coordinator-local.log","path":"tmp/ci-critical-path/replay-coordinator-local.log"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,85p' docs/ai-coding-reliability.md","name":"ai-coding-reliability.md","path":"docs/ai-coding-reliability.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"cat /Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/examples/good.md","name":"good.md","path":"/Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/examples/good.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"   Compiling digest v0.11.3\n   Compiling curve25519-dalek v5.0.0\n   Compiling errno v0.3.14\n   Compiling sqlcipher-src v419.0.6+sqlcipher-4.19.0-sqlite-3.53.4-libtomcrypt-1.18.2\n   Compiling utf8parse v0.2.2\n   Compiling smallvec v1.15.1\n   Compiling cpubits v0.1.1\n   Compiling parking_lot_core v0.9.11\n   Compiling adler2 v2.0.1\n   Compiling miniz_oxide v0.8.9\n   Compiling anstyle-parse v1.0.0\n   Compiling libsqlite3-sys v0.39.1\n# AI coding reliability guide\n\nUse this guide before implementing or reviewing a change, especially a bug fix,\nrefactor, merge resolution or optimization. Its purpose is to turn recurring\nfailure modes into concrete design questions and executable checks. Compiling,\nadding a timeout or passing a happy-path test does not establish reliability.\n\nThis guide owns the cross-cutting review method and reusable failure patterns.\n[Module ownership](module-boundaries.md) owns implementation boundaries;\n[architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md), [network contracts](network-design-reference.md)\nand feature guides own behavior and resource limits. The\n[testing workflow](testing-feedback-loop.md) owns commands, selection and gates.\nLink to those owners instead of copying their changing contracts here. When code\nand a contract disagree, resolve the intended behavior rather than documenting\nthe regression as the new rule.\n\n## Use on every change\n\n1. **Locate the owner and consumers.** Read the owning implementation, callers,\n   existing tests and relevant guide before adding another helper or state owner.\n   Include callbacks, IPC handlers, retries, startup, shutdown and recovery paths;\n   an import graph alone cannot reveal all consumers.\n2. **State the invariant and select risks.** Describe what must remain true on\n   success, failure, cancellation and replacement. Use the table below to select\n   applicable rules. A small diff can change lock order or lifetime semantics.\n3. **Trace the failure path before editing.** For concurrency work, sketch who\n   owns each lock, permit, task and mutable state; what it waits for; and who\n   releases it. For a reproducible bug, construct a focused failing regression.\n   Record unresolved hypotheses as hypotheses, not confirmed defects.\n4. **Implement at the existing boundary.** Preserve authority, durable acceptance,\n   compatibility and resource budgets. Recheck invariants after moving work\n   outside a guard. Evaluate the proposed fix for new races and deadlocks too.\n5. **Verify the changed boundary and report evidence.** Run affected checks and\n   required boundary scenarios under the testing workflow. Explain results,\n   exclusions and unresolved evidence. Update this guide when the defect exposes\n   a reusable failure pattern; follow the maintenance procedure below.\n\nKeep the risk explanation proportional to the change. A documentation edit does\nnot require a new concurrency test. A changed async callback does require a\nlifetime review even when the diff is one line. Put task-specific reasoning and\nresults in the PR or ignored local evidence, not a new permanent report.\n\n## Select rules by the changed boundary\n\n| Change or symptom                                                        | Read at least                                                                                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Locks, callbacks, synchronous dispatch, UI freezes                       | [R01](#r01-lock-order-and-reentrant-calls), [R02](#r02-thread-and-scheduler-progress), [R10](#r10-evidence-that-exercises-the-failure)                |\n| Background work, native providers, logout, shutdown or cancellation      | [R02](#r02-thread-and-scheduler-progress), [R03](#r03-actual-work-owns-its-lifetime), [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions)               |\n| Network requests, retries, polling, queues or batches                    | [R03](#r03-actual-work-owns-its-lifetime), [R04](#r04-deadlines-cover-the-whole-operation), [R05](#r05-bounded-work-must-also-make-progress)          |\n| React effects, IPC reads, dialogs, optimistic updates or mutation status | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R07](#r07-observation-is-not-mutation-ownership), [R10](#r10-evidence-that-exercises-the-failure) |\n| Storage, settings, imports, deletion or recovery                         | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R08](#r08-durable-state-and-failure-semantics), [R09](#r09-bound-input-and-resource-use)          |\n| IPC/API inputs, files, media or protocol changes                         | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R08](#r08-durable-state-and-failure-semantics), [R09](#r09-bound-input-and-resource-use)          |\n| Merge resolution, shared helper or dependency changes                    | Revisit the affected callers and all applicable rules; a clean textual merge does not establish compatible ownership or semantics.                    |\n\nThese are starting points, not permission to ignore another reachable boundary.\nDo not add a global lock, retry loop, timeout or shared abstraction solely to make\nthe local symptom disappear.\n\nA frozen UI may result from a lock cycle, blocked event thread, starvation, lost\nwakeup, unbounded work or stale loading state. Identify the mechanism and the\nmissing progress condition before selecting a fix; these need different evidence.\n\n## R01: Lock order and reentrant calls\n\n**Failure pattern:** one path takes A then B while another takes B then A; or a\nhelper/callback reacquires a nonrecursive lock its caller already holds. The\nsecond case can deadlock on a single thread. Nested reads also need review when\na queued writer can prevent a new read from completing.\n\nTrace actual acquisition order through callees, authorization callbacks,\ndestructors and error cleanup. Include main-thread dispatch and task joins as\nwait dependencies, not just mutexes. A typical UI cycle is:\n\n```text\nbackground task: holds session guard -> waits for main-thread callback\nmain thread:     handles UI event    -> waits for session guard\n```\n\nUse the owning module's order; do not invent one universal order for unrelated\nsubsystems. Make whether a helper acquires a guard or requires one explicit.\nAvoid invoking arbitrary callbacks while guarded. Where a commit callback must\nrun inside an authority guard, keep that contract narrow and inspect its complete\ncall chain. Queue presentation without retaining a guard needed by the UI;\nvalidate captured authority when the queued callback executes.\n\n# Good Commit Message Examples\n\nThese examples demonstrate the target quality for commit messages generated by this skill.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — Feature with clear scope\n\n```\nfeat(skill): add license and compatibility fields to all SKILL.md frontmatter\n```\n\n**Why it works:**\n- Subject is specific and action-oriented, under 72 chars\n- Body bullets map to real grouped changes, not generic \"update files\"\n- Scope `(skill)` accurately signals the affected area\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — Docs restructure\n\n```\ndocs: restructure standards into docs/ and unify AI entry points\n\n- move DEVELOPMENT.md sections into docs/skill-authoring.md,\n  docs/conventions.md, docs/testing.md, docs/workflow.md\n- replace CLAUDE.md body with pointer to AGENTS.md\n- add Quick Links table in AGENTS.md for per-task doc loading\n- keep DEVELOPMENT.md as overview only\n```\n\n**Why it works:**\n- Type `docs` is correct for non-code changes\n- Body explains the reorganization by action, not by file list\n- \"keep DEVELOPMENT.md as overview only\" captures the constraint decision\n\n---\n\n## Example 3 — Bug fix with concise body\n\n```\nfix(browser): handle stale CDP connection on cold Chrome start\n\n- detect empty tab list on connect and retry with 2s delay (max 3 times)\n- surface actionable error when port is open but Chrome is not ready\n- add test fixture for cold-start scenario\n```\n\n**Why it works:**\n- Subject names the exact symptom, not just \"fix bug\"\n- Body describes mechanism + error surface + test, no padding\n- Under 72 chars in subject\n\n---\n\n## Example 4 — Chore / housekeeping\n\n```\nchore: standardize skill structure and enforce Chinese language gate\n\n- add missing README.md and README.zh-CN.md to 6 skills\n- add .gitignore entry for .cache/ in browser skills\n- enforce no-Chinese rule via check:chinese npm script\n- add skills:validate script for post-change CI gate\n```\n\n**Why it works:**\n- `chore` type is correct (no production behavior change)\n- Groups related housekeeping into 4 concrete bullet points\n- Avoids vague \"clean up\", names what was actually done\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":9292}},"started_at_ms":1794157477442,"completed_at_ms":1794157477442}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-08T17:04:37.521Z","ordinal":44935,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1bc1e-7e91-7c91-b5d4-686911ac298a","call_id":"call_8edbe87fb216465eab9af4cab536349d","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"47484e\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000009292,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2203,\"output\":\"   Compiling digest v0.11.3\\n   Compiling curve25519-dalek v5.0.0\\n   Compiling errno v0.3.14\\n   Compiling sqlcipher-src v419.0.6+sqlcipher-4.19.0-sqlite-3.53.4-libtomcrypt-1.18.2\\n   Compiling utf8parse v0.2.2\\n   Compiling smallvec v1.15.1\\n   Compiling cpubits v0.1.1\\n   Compiling parking_lot_core v0.9.11\\n   Compiling adler2 v2.0.1\\n   Compiling miniz_oxide v0.8.9\\n   Compiling anstyle-parse v1.0.0\\n   Compiling libsqlite3-sys v0.39.1\\n# AI coding reliability guide\\n\\nUse this guide before implementing or reviewing a change, especially a bug fix,\\nrefactor, merge resolution or optimization. Its purpose is to turn recurring\\nfailure modes into concrete design questions and executable checks. Compiling,\\nadding a timeout or passing a happy-path test does not establish reliability.\\n\\nThis guide owns the cross-cutting review method and reusable failure patterns.\\n[Module ownership](module-boundaries.md) owns implementation boundaries;\\n[architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md), [network contracts](network-design-reference.md)\\nand feature guides own behavior and resource limits. The\\n[testing workflow](testing-feedback-loop.md) owns commands, selection and gates.\\nLink to those owners instead of copying their changing contracts here. When code\\nand a contract disagree, resolve the intended behavior rather than documenting\\nthe regression as the new rule.\\n\\n## Use on every change\\n\\n1. **Locate the owner and consumers.** Read the owning implementation, callers,\\n   existing tests and relevant guide before adding another helper or state owner.\\n   Include callbacks, IPC handlers, retries, startup, shutdown and recovery paths;\\n   an import graph alone cannot reveal all consumers.\\n2. **State the invariant and select risks.** Describe what must remain true on\\n   success, failure, cancellation and replacement. Use the table below to select\\n   applicable rules. A small diff can change lock order or lifetime semantics.\\n3. **Trace the failure path before editing.** For concurrency work, sketch who\\n   owns each lock, permit, task and mutable state; what it waits for; and who\\n   releases it. For a reproducible bug, construct a focused failing regression.\\n   Record unresolved hypotheses as hypotheses, not confirmed defects.\\n4. **Implement at the existing boundary.** Preserve authority, durable acceptance,\\n   compatibility and resource budgets. Recheck invariants after moving work\\n   outside a guard. Evaluate the proposed fix for new races and deadlocks too.\\n5. **Verify the changed boundary and report evidence.** Run affected checks and\\n   required boundary scenarios under the testing workflow. Explain results,\\n   exclusions and unresolved evidence. Update this guide when the defect exposes\\n   a reusable failure pattern; follow the maintenance procedure below.\\n\\nKeep the risk explanation proportional to the change. A documentation edit does\\nnot require a new concurrency test. A changed async callback does require a\\nlifetime review even when the diff is one line. Put task-specific reasoning and\\nresults in the PR or ignored local evidence, not a new permanent report.\\n\\n## Select rules by the changed boundary\\n\\n| Change or symptom                                                        | Read at least                                                                                                                                         |\\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Locks, callbacks, synchronous dispatch, UI freezes                       | [R01](#r01-lock-order-and-reentrant-calls), [R02](#r02-thread-and-scheduler-progress), [R10](#r10-evidence-that-exercises-the-failure)                |\\n| Background work, native providers, logout, shutdown or cancellation      | [R02](#r02-thread-and-scheduler-progress), [R03](#r03-actual-work-owns-its-lifetime), [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions)               |\\n| Network requests, retries, polling, queues or batches                    | [R03](#r03-actual-work-owns-its-lifetime), [R04](#r04-deadlines-cover-the-whole-operation), [R05](#r05-bounded-work-must-also-make-progress)          |\\n| React effects, IPC reads, dialogs, optimistic updates or mutation status | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R07](#r07-observation-is-not-mutation-ownership), [R10](#r10-evidence-that-exercises-the-failure) |\\n| Storage, settings, imports, deletion or recovery                         | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R08](#r08-durable-state-and-failure-semantics), [R09](#r09-bound-input-and-resource-use)          |\\n| IPC/API inputs, files, media or protocol changes                         | [R06](#r06-exact-authority-and-stale-completions), [R08](#r08-durable-state-and-failure-semantics), [R09](#r09-bound-input-and-resource-use)          |\\n| Merge resolution, shared helper or dependency changes                    | Revisit the affected callers and all applicable rules; a clean textual merge does not establish compatible ownership or semantics.                    |\\n\\nThese are starting points, not permission to ignore another reachable boundary.\\nDo not add a global lock, retry loop, timeout or shared abstraction solely to make\\nthe local symptom disappear.\\n\\nA frozen UI may result from a lock cycle, blocked event thread, starvation, lost\\nwakeup, unbounded work or stale loading state. Identify the mechanism and the\\nmissing progress condition before selecting a fix; these need different evidence.\\n\\n## R01: Lock order and reentrant calls\\n\\n**Failure pattern:** one path takes A then B while another takes B then A; or a\\nhelper/callback reacquires a nonrecursive lock its caller already holds. The\\nsecond case can deadlock on a single thread. Nested reads also need review when\\na queued writer can prevent a new read from completing.\\n\\nTrace actual acquisition order through callees, authorization callbacks,\\ndestructors and error cleanup. Include main-thread dispatch and task joins as\\nwait dependencies, not just mutexes. A typical UI cycle is:\\n\\n```text\\nbackground task: holds session guard -> waits for main-thread callback\\nmain thread:     handles UI event    -> waits for session guard\\n```\\n\\nUse the owning module's order; do not invent one universal order for unrelated\\nsubsystems. Make whether a helper acquires a guard or requires one explicit.\\nAvoid invoking arbitrary callbacks while guarded. Where a commit callback must\\nrun inside an authority guard, keep that contract narrow and inspect its complete\\ncall chain. Queue presentation without retaining a guard needed by the UI;\\nvalidate captured authority when the queued callback executes.\\n\\n# Good Commit Message Examples\\n\\nThese examples demonstrate the target quality for commit messages generated by this skill.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Example 1 — Feature with clear scope\\n\\n```\\nfeat(skill): add license and compatibility fields to all SKILL.md frontmatter\\n```\\n\\n**Why it works:**\\n- Subject is specific and action-oriented, under 72 chars\\n- Body bullets map to real grouped changes, not generic \\\"update files\\\"\\n- Scope `(skill)` accurately signals the affected area\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Example 2 — Docs restructure\\n\\n```\\ndocs: restructure standards into docs/ and unify AI entry points\\n\\n- move DEVELOPMENT.md sections into docs/skill-authoring.md,\\n  docs/conventions.md, docs/testing.md, docs/workflow.md\\n- replace CLAUDE.md body with pointer to AGENTS.md\\n- add Quick Links table in AGENTS.md for per-task doc loading\\n- keep DEVELOPMENT.md as overview only\\n```\\n\\n**Why it works:**\\n- Type `docs` is correct for non-code changes\\n- Body explains the reorganization by action, not by file list\\n- \\\"keep DEVELOPMENT.md as overview only\\\" captures the constraint decision\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Example 3 — Bug fix with concise body\\n\\n```\\nfix(browser): handle stale CDP connection on cold Chrome start\\n\\n- detect empty tab list on connect and retry with 2s delay (max 3 times)\\n- surface actionable error when port is open but Chrome is not ready\\n- add test fixture for cold-start scenario\\n```\\n\\n**Why it works:**\\n- Subject names the exact symptom, not just \\\"fix bug\\\"\\n- Body describes mechanism + error surface + test, no padding\\n- Under 72 chars in subject\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Example 4 — Chore / housekeeping\\n\\n```\\nchore: standardize skill structure and enforce Chinese language gate\\n\\n- add missing README.md and README.zh-CN.md to 6 skills\\n- add .gitignore entry for .cache/ in browser skills\\n- enforce no-Chinese rule via check:chinese npm script\\n- add skills:validate script for post-change CI gate\\n```\\n\\n**Why it works:**\\n- `chore` type is correct (no production behavior change)\\n- Groups related housekeeping into 4 concrete bullet points\\n- Avoids vague \\\"clean up\\\", names what was actually done\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1b8a7-3fc8-7460-a590-a3bb58793d59","create_time":1794157477.521518}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
